83
00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:09.020
Fast fact number one, we had a lot of questions about the convention center, the convention

84
00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:09.500
itself.

85
00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.040
A lot of curiosity.

86
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:20.200
For example, fan question from Mary Ann Lodens and Grace D and Mel Asako, where was the speech

87
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:20.640
filmed?

88
00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:27.060
And then Sue Potts asked, did we hire a bunch of extras or did we just use existing hotel

89
00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:27.540
people?

90
00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.480
Well, I reached out to our line producer, Kent Zabornak.

91
00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.540
So a line producer is the person who gets the script and figures out all of the practical

92
00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:41.620
stuff that you need to do to make an episode happen.

93
00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:48.440
So whereas Greg Daniels is in charge of all the creative aspects of an episode, Kent Zabornak

94
00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:49.740
would be in charge of the practicals.

95
00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.200
And the logistics.

96
00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:51.600
Yes.

97
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:52.720
How do I get people there?

98
00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:53.620
Where are we filming?

99
00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:54.660
What does it cost?

100
00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:55.140
Yes.

101
00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.440
How do we make this work with our budget?

102
00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:58.360
All of that kind of stuff.

103
00:03:58.380 --> 00:03:58.580
Yes.

104
00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:03.420
For example, he would be a person who would give Phil Shea a budget for 13 fitness orbs.

105
00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.740
He'd be like, here's your fitness orb budget.

106
00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.620
So he's in charge of all those numbers and things.

107
00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:14.340
He said that we filmed for only one day at the Universal Studios Sheridan Hotel.

108
00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:23.540
Now, I had written in my blog a while ago that we had 500 extras, but Kent said we did not

109
00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:24.860
have 500 extras.

110
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.700
We had 400 extras.

111
00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:28.540
Yes.

112
00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.460
And 100 dummies.

113
00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:31.960
Bodies?

114
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:32.640
Yes.

115
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:33.500
Fake bodies.

116
00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:37.980
Torsos with no arms and legs, dressed up in suits.

117
00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:38.620
Yeah.

118
00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:44.420
And five, dummy wranglers, people who carried them around the room and put them in different

119
00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:46.240
places depending on the shots.

120
00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:48.960
I mean, that is crazy town.

121
00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:50.160
That is crazy town.

122
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.100
I did have in my notes that we had about 400 extras, and it's the most extras we had ever

123
00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:55.440
had.

124
00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.040
Yes, this is true.

125
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.620
So Kent said that the dummies were mostly in the back and on the sides.

126
00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:04.080
Yes.

127
00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.840
He said if you pause and look, you can find them.

128
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.040
But I went frame by frame, and I could not find any.

129
00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.220
You know, the room was really—

130
00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:13.580
I mean, I really looked.

131
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:14.050
Yeah.

132
00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:20.280
So there were only three cast members that went to the convention, and it was, you know,

133
00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:25.120
Rain, obviously, Dwight's speech, and then Michael, and myself.

134
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:25.760
Mm-hmm.

135
00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:32.160
The room was actually really big, and if my memory serves, they had the dummies sort of

136
00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.840
way towards the back, so it just looked like the room was filled all the way to the back.

137
00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:36.260
Mm-hmm.

138
00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:38.300
I wouldn't think that you could catch it.

139
00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:39.420
I think that'd be pretty hard.

140
00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:44.500
He mentioned that Charles McDougal was really worried that you were going to see these dummies.

141
00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:44.920
Yeah.

142
00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:48.820
And so the Wranglers really moved them around a lot.

143
00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:49.960
The dummy Wranglers.

144
00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:51.020
The dummy Wranglers.

145
00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.180
What's interesting to me—

146
00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:53.780
Is that our book?

147
00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.380
The Dummy Wranglers.

148
00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.640
No, that's Kent Sabornat's book.

149
00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:57.940
Yeah.

150
00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.800
About dealing with us.

151
00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:00.100
Yeah.

152
00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:02.700
There were a lot of extras.

153
00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:05.220
There were—it was the most I had ever seen.

154
00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:09.600
We had never really had to provide lunch for that many people.

155
00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.700
Like, I remember when it came time for lunch, like, the catering was way—it was like Sergio

156
00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.320
plus all these other extra guys.

157
00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:16.610
Mm-hmm.

158
00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:17.600
That was our catering company.

159
00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:23.560
So it was kind of interesting to see how we managed that many people, and I was glad we

